[00:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Real influencers are being replaced by AI creators that pull millions of views every single month. So, I made three by myself and ran them across the most popular niches to see if this actually works for every single one. In this video, I\'ll show you the full process of'

[00:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'making an AI influencer that stays consistent across every video, as well as how to make content with it, so you get your own creator that performs for you without you ever having to show your'

[00:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'face. I\'ll start with the one thing most people miss, and it\'s the reason their AI influencer makes viewers click off before the most important part of the video. This is what happens when they try to use different concepts in the'

[00:37] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'video, like outfits or environments, because it\'s harder to keep your characters look consistent with all those changing elements. So, before I generate anything, I\'ll lock my character\'s face into a reference'

[00:49] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '[music] sheet I\'ll use in every generation so it stays consistent throughout [music] the whole video. And I\'ll use Higsfield for that throughout the video because the images, the [music] character, the footage, and the voice are all on one platform. So, I can'

[01:02] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'make everything in one place. I\'ll leave a link to it in the description if you want to follow along. For this generation, I\'ll be using Nano Banana Pro because it\'s one of the best tools for creating images. So, once I\'m'

[01:15] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '[music] in Higsfield, I\'ll head to the image tab on the left and select it. Now, the mistake almost everyone [music] makes is that they upload a few photos of themselves, hit generate, and let the model put everything together. What it'

[01:27] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'actually does is blend each into one generic influencer face that doesn\'t look like the person in the photos at all. This next part matters more than anything else in the step. Now, I\'ll put four real photos of me there, and I\'ll'

[01:40] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'tell the model that this is one real woman, so it will not composite or average them. If one shot is clearer than [music] the rest. It should focus on it to make it look as accurate as possible. I\'ll [music] set the aspect'

[01:52] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'ratio to 16x9. Add those photos. Paste the prompt and hit generate. So now I\'ve got a four panel sheet of my character. [music] Front, 3/4, side, and a closeup on the face all on that plain white'

[02:05] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'background with real skin texture. And it genuinely looks just like me. So I\'m happy with the result. And every prompt I\'ll use in this whole video is linked down in the description [music] so you'

[02:17] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'can use them and follow along easily. That\'s the face locked. And from here, every prompt just tells the model to match this sheet. But even with my face locked in, the influencer still doesn\'t give us what we want yet. So to make'

[02:29] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'this actually look real, now I\'ll build the environment my character is going to be in. And the whole point of building the room now before any video is that once it\'s locked, the video only has to handle my character\'s performance'

[02:42] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'without having to guess the location\'s [music] look and its lighting at the same time. The reason most rooms come out looking fake [music] is the prompt itself. People type make it cinematic or make it look real, and the model hands'

[02:54] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'back generic lighting and a look that obviously seems AI generated. What actually works is naming [music] the exact thing you want rather than its mood. So instead of calling it cinematic, I name the exact grade I'

[03:06] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'want. Warm amber shadows, cool blue gay midtones, [music] and a Kodak Vision 3250D film look with a bit of 35 mm grain. [music] The lighting works the'

[03:18] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'same. So I\'ll put a real lamp in the room, switch it on, and let that warm [music] glow be the actual light source instead of generic brightness. And when I want [music] it to look real, I don\'t tell the model to make it look real, but I describe it more clearly, [music]'

[03:31] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'saying that this should be a photo shot on a real camera, not a 3D render and not [music] a video game. On top of that, I\'ll give my character a normal table at a normal height with real wood grain. And I\'ll leave open floor in front of it, so my character actually'

[03:44] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'has enough space to stand and film. The pallet image I attach is only there for the colors and the furniture. So, I\'ll tell it not to copy that room exactly. [music] Just match the feel in a new layout. And I\'m still in Nano Banana Pro'

[03:57] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'on the [music] same 16x9 sheet with the room empty of people and just a bed in [music] the background. Then I\'ll paste the whole thing in and generate. And I like how cozy this one looks, especially with the lit lamp on the nightstand.'

[04:10] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '[music] So, now I\'ve got the room locked. And from now on, the video keeps my character in the same looking space. But there\'s still one problem we haven\'t solved [music] yet, which is why my character might sound like AI. So now'

[04:22] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'I\'ll give my character a specific script to fix this. [music] Almost every AI influencer script sounds the same. Greeting the viewer at the beginning or saying that they\'re excited to show something to the viewer, [music] which'

[04:34] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'sounds fake right away. To make it feel realistic, my character should sound like someone who wasn\'t even sure they\'d like the thing. So, I\'ll build every script on the same four parts, and they\'re the reason it comes across as a person rather than an ad. It opens with'

[04:47] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'my character being skeptical, saying something like, "This jacket sat in my cart for 2 weeks before I finally opened it." Then, my character points out one specific real detail, like the stitching'

[04:59] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'being way better than expected. There\'s also one honest flaw, like the sleeves running long if you\'ve got shorter arms. and it closes with a verdict that sounds like a real opinion, which is what actually makes it sound reliable. The'

[05:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'other thing I\'ll do is write my character\'s physical actions into the script in brackets so they line up with the spoken words and it all feels more realistic. And now comes the part everything else was building toward.'

[05:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Taking that locked face, that room, and this script, and turning all of it into the actual video. So, I\'ll switch over to the video tab in the top bar next to the image tab and pick [music] seedance 2.5 from the model list. The reason I\'m'

[05:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'using this one is that seed dance 2.5 can generate up to 30 seconds in a single take using my reference images and generating the audio at the same time. So, instead of making six short'

[05:50] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'clips and stitching them together, I can generate the entire review as one continuous take, which keeps my character\'s face and voice consistent throughout. So, I\'ll add the character sheet and the room as my references, and'

[06:03] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'I\'ll leave the audio on so my character actually speaks inside the video. In the [music] prompt, I\'ll tell it to make my character match the character sheet exactly. Same with the room, table, lamp, and grade from the environment.'

[06:17] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'And also, I\'ll tell it to give the camera a real handheld feel and split it into segments, one for each action in my script, so my character never talks and moves at the same time. And I\'ll tell it her voice has to sound like a real warm'

[06:30] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'person, not robotic or synthetic because [music] that\'s what usually gives an AI video away. For the settings, I\'ll set it to 9x6 for a vertical video. resolution to 1080p, the duration'

[06:42] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '[music] to full 30 seconds, and I\'ll leave the bit rate on standard because high barely looks any different and just takes [music] longer to generate. Then I\'ll paste the prompt and see how it runs. Okay, so this jacket has been sitting in my cart for like 2 weeks and'

